  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 235 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nFriday through Saturday... As the shortwave moves east-\nsoutheast across Iowa, showers and thunderstorms will develop \nalong the nose of the LLJ in western and central Iowa, reaching \nthe western edge of the CWA by 10-12Z. Marginal instability of \nup to 500 J/kg of MUCAPE will support embedded storms within the\nshowers, especially south of Interstate 80, through the \nmorning. The potential for any severe storms will be limited \nthrough the morning due to to weak instability. Come afternoon, \nsome clearing across southeast Iowa, northeast Missouri, and \nwest- central Illinois will help increase MUCAPE to 1000-1500 \nJ/kg. This, combined with 0-6km shear of 30-35 kts, could \nsupport isolated strong to severe thunderstorms Friday \nafternoon with the primary threats being localized damaging \nwinds and isolated tornadoes.\n\nShowers and storms will continue overnight Friday into Saturday. \nThis system will be slow to exit the area, resulting in lingering \nshowers through Saturday morning to afternoon. Given the ample \nmoisture available, rainfall accumulations continue to trend \nupwards. At least 1 inch of rain is highly likely (NBM 80-90%), and \nthe chances of 2 inches or more from the NBM are now at 40-70% from \nsouthwest to northeast. This will be quite welcome due to the recent \ndry conditions, though heavy rain rates over a short period could\nresult in localized flooding concerns, especially in urban areas.\n\nLastly, as the shortwave departs Saturday, a tight pressure gradient \nwill cause gusty winds up to 30 mph through the evening. \n\n\nSunday through Thursday... Following the passage of the \nshortwave, a ridge will briefly take hold over the region before\nthe flow aloft becomes more zonal. Of note is a deep trough \nover southern Canada through much of next week, which will send \nimpulses through the flow and provide multiple chances for \nrainfall.",
